\subsection{History size and missingness}\label{app:history}
Selection with a smaller dense history degrades slowly (Figure~\ref{fig:history}). By the point estimate (three repeats; by the upper interval limit MTEB-Eng needs half), a quarter of the history models (about 29) stays within 10\% of the full-history RMSE on MTEB-Eng, half on MTEB-Multi, and all models on HELM MMLU; randomly missing history cells down to half the cells stay within 10\% on all three pools, but missingness concentrated on weaker models does not at any density tried (0.3 to 0.7).

\subsection{Sparse leaderboard matrix}\label{app:sparse}
The six subsets keep the real missing cells of the MTEB score matrix. \emph{English, 21\%} holds the 445 models and 335 English tasks reported for at least 20 models (a median of 60 observed cells per model; 337 models have at least 30). The other subsets are greedy trimmings of the English tasks (332$\times$295, 268$\times$176 and 226$\times$134 at 30, 50 and 70\% observed) and of all tasks (331$\times$705 and 236$\times$517 at 30 and 50\%); the trimmed subsets are one greedy choice among many. Models are grouped by family and held out in ten folds as in the dense pools. A training column needs at least 10 observed training cells and is standardised on its observed training cells; only the identity transform is used. A model with at least 30 observed cells is a test model; for each of three random partitions, 30\% of its observed cells (rounded) are the targets and the remaining cells are the candidates, from which the selector chooses; the predictor uses only the selected cells. Greedy A uses the masked-EM factor-model covariance; random selection averages 30 draws per $k$ (the dense runs average 50 to 200). The factor model chooses its rank by five-fold held-out masked marginal likelihood and the one-standard-error rule. The pairwise predictor takes pairwise-complete correlations, clips them to a positive semi-definite matrix and shrinks them toward the identity, with the shrinkage in $\{0.02,\dots,0.4\}$ chosen on training rows. A masked Soft-Impute fold-in is only slightly better than the column mean (its fold-in shrinks the cells it cannot see) and is omitted from Table~\ref{tab:sparse}. Standardised RMSE is the square root of the mean over test models of the per-model mean squared error. Closure is $(e_{\rm colmean}-e_k)/(e_{\rm colmean}-e_{\rm LOBO})$ with $e_{\rm LOBO}$ the lower error of the two predictors conditioning on all candidates; this reference is itself noisy (interval about $\pm0.4$), so closure is noisier than the gap. The grid is $k=1,\dots,6,8,10,12,15,20$: a model has too few candidate cells for larger $k$. Intervals are family-clustered bootstraps ($B=2000$), and $k^*$ is censored when the level is not reached on the grid. Figure~\ref{fig:sparse} and Appendix Table~\ref{tab:sparse-k} give closure and the paired gap at every $k$ of the grid.

